{"text": "Digital [Ds to end online\nmob rule and anonymity\n\nMark Bridge, Richard Ford,\nRachel Sylvester, Alice Thomson\n\nDigital IDs should be introduced to re-\nmove online anonymity and end inter-\nnet \u201cmob rule\u201d, the security minister\nhas told The Times.\nBen Wallace, a former soldier, said\nbullying and grooming occurred on\nsocial media because offenders be-\nlieved they cannot be identified. \u201cIt is\nmob rule on the internet. You shouldn't\nbe able to hide behind anonymity as\nmuch as you can now,\u201d he added.\nHe said that banks can authenticate\ntheir users\u2019 identities online, so there is\nno reason other websites could not do\nthe same.  \u2014 \u00e9 .\n\u201cI remember going to see an under-\ncover online [investigation] into child\nsex exploitation. There was a children\u2019s\nchat room with a 45-year-old pretend-\ning to be a 12-year-old. It was like blood\nin the water with a shark...If we're\ngoing to make the internet safer, and\ncut out the abuse, we're going to have to\ndo something more about some form of\n\nof violence against women and girls.\nExperts said a digital ID system could\nbe based on the existing Verify system\nfor government services such as the\nHMRC website. Users show docu-\nments such as their passport and\ndriver\u2019 licence to a partner organisa-\ntion, like the Post Office, to validate\ntheir identity and create an online ac-\ncount. A text is sent to reconfirm their\nidentity whenever they log in.\nAlternatively, a scheme could be\nbased on the forthcoming age-verifica-\ntion procedure for pornographic sites,\nwhere regulated companies create\ntools to check website users\u2019 identities.\nHowever, Verify requires ID docu-\nments that some people do not possess,\nwhile age verification for pornography\nhias been delayed amid concerns over\n\nOpinion is\nscheme could be effective. Alan Wood-\nward, of the University, of Surrey, said:\n\"| think it\u2019s technically workable.\n\ndon't\nFor those who persist in the vilest on-\nline behaviour, there are so many ways\nto mask their real identities.\u201d\n\nisa threat to our national security of the\nfirst order. Legal compulsion will be\nnecessary and we should go for it.\u201d\nMr Wallace also slapped down Gavin\nWilliamson, the defence secretary, for\nsaying British Isis fighters should be\n\u201chunted down and killed\u201d to ensure\nthat they never return to the UK.\nThe comments were criticised at the\ntime for being \u201cjuvenile\u201d and Mr Wal-\nlace made little attempt to disguise his\ndisdain for his colleague's view. \u201cThey\nshould all face justice for the crimes\nthey did. This country doesn't believe in\nthe death penalty, we don't execute\npeople,\u201d he said. He added: \u201cIf you arein\nalwar it is a different issue, we will deal\nwith whatever we can deal with,\nwherever the threat is, but we are Brit-\nish. We don't set out to wipe people out.\nWe set out to make us secure\u201d.\nAsked if Mr Williamson had been ir-\nresponsible to suggest a dead terrorist\ncan't cause any harm to Britain , Mr\nWallace said laughingly: \u201cYou will have\nto ask Gavin Williamson\u201d, .\nMr Wallace, who served in N\u00a2\n\nIreland, said: \u201cI've tote,\nterrorist\nSpeen hhav\u00ae xillsomeone andtobe\nhastater} that and no one |\nknow takesit lightly. No one should rel-\nish it\u201d\n\nsacatae iene teas 0085 3435",
